<h2>The text and its translation</h2>
<div class="texte-latin">
    <div class="entete lat">Latin</div>
    <div class="entete fr">English</div>
    <div class="lat">Alleluia.</div>
    <div class="fr">Alleluia.</div>
    <div class="lat"><span class="chip">Verse</span> Dies sanctificatus illuxit nobis&nbsp;:<br>venite gentes, et adorate Dominum&nbsp;:</div>
    <div class="fr">A sanctified day has shone upon us&nbsp;;<br>come, you nations, and adore the Lord&nbsp;;</div>
    <div class="lat">quia hodie descendit lux magna super terram.</div>
    <div class="fr">for today a great light has come down upon the earth.</div>
</div>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Definition — Jubilus</span>
    <p>
        The <span class="mot-latin">jubilus</span> is the long vocalise that prolongs the
        final «&nbsp;a&nbsp;» of <span class="mot-latin">Alleluia</span>. Saint Augustine
        gives it its finest definition&nbsp;: the song of a heart too full to find its
        words. It is the <strong>melisma</strong> in its pure state — the notes no longer
        carry a text, but a joy.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>How an Alleluia is built</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    An Alleluia is not a chant «&nbsp;in a straight line&nbsp;»&nbsp;: it is a
    there-and-back. The soloist launches the word <span class="mot-latin">Alleluia</span>,
    the choir takes it up and prolongs it with the jubilus&nbsp;; the soloist then sings the
    <strong>verse</strong>&nbsp;; then everyone returns to the starting point, jubilus
    included. This <strong>responsorial</strong> form — a soloist, a response — is exactly
    the one we met in the heritage of psalmody.
</p>

<h3>What that changes for reading</h3>
<ul>
    <li>
The text is <strong>tiny</strong> (one word, then a short verse)&nbsp;: all the attention
goes to the drawing of the neumes, not to the syllables.
</li>
    <li>
The jubilus is read like any melisma&nbsp;: you <strong>cut it up</strong> into familiar
neumes — pes, clivis, climacus, quilisma.
</li>
    <li>
The return of the jubilus after the verse offers a rare opportunity&nbsp;: comparing
<strong>the same line of neumes twice</strong>, a few centimetres apart.
</li>
</ul>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">The role of the old notations here</span>
    <p>
        In such a long vocalise, square notation gives the notes but not the breath. It is
        the layers of <strong>Laon</strong> (above) and <strong>St. Gall</strong> (below)
        that cut the jubilus into breathable little units&nbsp;: a <strong>c</strong> (<span
        class="mot-latin">celeriter</span>) to flow on, a <strong>t</strong> (<span
        class="mot-latin">tenete</span>) or an episema to settle. Without them, the jubilus
        is sung flat.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Did you know — one Alleluia for several feasts</span>
    <p>
        Alleluia melodies travel&nbsp;: one and the same melodic line often serves several
        verses through the liturgical year. The Alleluia repertoire is thus a wonderful
        field for observing the <strong>reuse</strong> of melodies — a common procedure in
        chant, where a known model is adapted to a new text.
    </p>
</div>

